      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/69e1bae966baea90d6f2395bd4db263d3a564f53912f67d4e0812243d28550e3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta = 100^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane. What is its reference angle $\\theta_R?$",
      "worked_solution": "The reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R,$  is the angle that $\\theta=100^\\circ$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/e453e8a82b70d69009db59f161a798cb57c85602d79b4bcc44757cd2a192b860.png)\n\nSince $\\theta$ is in the second quadrant, its reference angle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{100}^{∘} \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
